---
import { getCollection, getEntry } from "astro:content";
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

const services = (await getCollection("services")).sort((a, b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
const aiWorkflowArticle = await getEntry("writing", "design-system-marketing-engine");
const mcpArticle = await getEntry("writing", "mcp-project-brains");
const mcpArticleHref = mcpArticle?.data.path ?? mcpArticle?.data.canonicalUrl;
---

<BaseLayout title="Services" description="Android contract and tech-lead work, mobile and web product builds, and Android codebase reviews from Stoyan Korudzhiev, a Senior Software Engineer with 7+ years of production Android experience.">
  <header class="page-intro services-page-intro shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">How I can help</p>
    <h1>Senior Android and frontend help, hands-on or as a lead.</h1>
    <p class="page-lede">I've spent 7+ years shipping production Android apps in fintech, health tech, and iLottery, about two of them as an Android tech lead, and I now work in Vue, TypeScript, and Phaser too. Here are three concrete ways I can help. If your situation fits none of them, <a href="/start/">tell me about it anyway</a>.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="page-section services-page-section shell services-stack">
    {services.map((service) => (
      <article class="service-detail">
        <div><p class="eyebrow">{service.data.eyebrow}</p><h2>{service.data.title}</h2></div>
        <div class="service-detail-copy">
          <p>{service.data.outcome}</p>
          <div class="service-detail-lists">
            <div><h3>Useful when</h3><ul>{service.data.bestFor.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul></div>
            <div><h3>Typical outputs</h3><ul>{service.data.deliverables.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}</ul></div>
          </div>
          <p><strong>Format:</strong> {service.data.engagement}</p>
        </div>
      </article>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="context-systems-section" id="context-systems">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="context-systems-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Also: AI and MCP integrations</p>
          <h2>Give AI the right context.<br />Keep the boundary visible.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>I build and operationalize MCP integrations that connect AI assistants to project knowledge, real workflows, and human-approved actions. The protocol is only the interface; the product still needs clear state, permissions, review, and failure behavior.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="context-systems-grid">
        <article class="context-system-offer">
          <span>Project brain + MCP</span>
          <h3>Context that survives the conversation.</h3>
          <p>A project brain keeps the mission, evidence, constraints, decisions, progress, and outcomes attached to the product. MCP exposes only the context and actions a workflow actually needs.</p>
          <ul>
            <li>Project-scoped knowledge and source ingestion</li>
            <li>Typed MCP tools, context contracts, and integration boundaries</li>
            <li>Proposal, review, confirmation, and audit flows</li>
            <li>Packaging, health checks, tests, and operational handoff</li>
          </ul>
          {mcpArticle && mcpArticleHref && (
            <a class="text-link" href={mcpArticleHref}>
              Read the field note <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </a>
          )}
        </article>

        <div class="context-system-evidence" aria-label="MCP integration examples">
          <article>
            <span>Continuum</span>
            <h3>Local product integration</h3>
            <p>A development-only MCP boundary can validate and export declarative product models or stage a change for review. The running application—not the protocol client—owns approval.</p>
          </article>
          <article>
            <span>Postiz Chat Bridge</span>
            <h3>Remote operations workflow</h3>
            <p>A private Marketing Brain grounds campaign and profile work in project evidence, while external publishing actions remain separated behind preview, immutable approval, and explicit confirmation.</p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p class="context-systems-boundary"><strong>Where this stands:</strong> I've built these integrations for my own products. If you need something similar, it fits best as part of a build or review engagement.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="ai-delivery-section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="ai-delivery-heading">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">How I use AI</p>
          <h2>Models in the loop.<br />Judgment at the center.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>I use AI to shorten the path from an ambiguous question to a reviewed, useful output. The leverage comes from fitting models into a clear product workflow—not asking them to replace context, verification, or responsibility.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="ai-delivery-grid">
        <article class="ai-delivery-step">
          <span>01 · Explore</span>
          <h3>Turn raw context into options.</h3>
          <p>Synthesize research, test alternative directions, and create rapid prototypes before committing to the expensive path.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="ai-delivery-step">
          <span>02 · Build</span>
          <h3>Accelerate the critical path.</h3>
          <p>Use engineering assistance for implementation, failure-case exploration, QA, tests, and documentation—then verify it against the real product.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="ai-delivery-step">
          <span>03 · Operate</span>
          <h3>Make repeatable work repeatable.</h3>
          <p>Connect content variation, launch assets, and production steps into workflows with explicit review and approval points.</p>
        </article>
      </div>

      <div class="ai-delivery-footer">
        <p><strong>The guardrail:</strong> meaningful decisions and shipped output remain human-reviewed.</p>
        {aiWorkflowArticle && (
          <a class="text-link" href={aiWorkflowArticle.data.canonicalUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            See an AI-assisted production system in practice <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
          </a>
        )}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="closing-cta services-closing-cta">
    <div class="shell closing-cta-grid">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">Next step</p><h2>Tell me what you need.</h2><p>A few sentences about the role, product, or codebase are enough. I reply to every relevant message by email.</p></div>
      <a class="button" href="/start/">Work with me <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
